# Normalize on commit, check out with LF everywhere.
#
# This repository is byte-sensitive in ways most are not:
#
#   * `qqq-pkg::store` compares content digests, and `qqq-lock`/`qqq.lock`
#     carries a covering hash over its own bytes. A file that changes line
#     endings between checkout and commit produces a different digest for
#     the same logical content.
#   * fixtures and expected-output files are asserted against exactly.
#   * the reproducibility claim (REL/REP items in the Checklist) is only
#     meaningful if the bytes on disk are the bytes that were committed.
#
# `core.autocrlf` should also be `false` in this repository, and is not by
# default on Windows:
#
#     git config core.autocrlf false
#
# It is a *contributing* factor rather than the cause, and that distinction cost
# two wrong diagnoses (§O-041). The cause is the `text=auto` above interacting
# with the checkout path; `autocrlf=false` is hygiene that removes one more
# thing that could rewrite a file. CONTRIBUTING.md lists it as a setup step,
# because a per-repository setting cannot be committed.
#
# `tools/normalize_eol.py --check` runs in CI and fails if a tracked text file
# would check out with the wrong endings, so a regression is caught rather than
# rediscovered. It also reports a non-`false` `core.autocrlf` loudly, because
# that state means the drift will return even when every file currently reads LF.
#
# The default: everything is text and gets LF.
#
# `text=auto` rather than `text` on the wildcard: forcing `text` on every file
# would corrupt a binary that is not caught by an explicit rule below.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The three canonical documents are pinned with `text`, NOT `text=auto`.
#
# This distinction is the actual fix for the drift, and it took a bisect to
# find. With `text=auto`, Git *auto-detects* whether a file is text and applies
# `eol=lf` as a consequence of that decision — and on a Windows checkout the
# detection path rewrites these files to CRLF when it refreshes the index. The
# result was `i/lf w/crlf`, with `git status` reporting three files modified
# and `git diff` showing nothing.
#
# `text` states the answer instead of asking Git to work it out, so there is no
# detection step to get wrong. Measured: with `text=auto`, `git status` on a
# stale index rewrote all three; with `text`, repeated `git add -A` and
# `git status` cycles leave the tree at zero non-LF files.
#
# These three are pinned individually rather than by extension because the
# *other* Markdown in the repository (crates/*/README.md, docs/adr/README.md)
# has never drifted, and widening a rule to fix a symptom it does not explain
# is how a fix becomes a new bug.
/QQQ-Proposal-V1.md              text eol=lf
/QQQ-Checklist-V1.md             text eol=lf
/QQQ-Observations-and-Memories.md text eol=lf

# Binary formats must never be touched. `text=auto` already detects most,
# but these three are called out because they are load-bearing:
#   - `.wasm`/`.cwasm` are compiled artifacts whose digests are compared.
#   - `.wit` is text, but it is listed as text on purpose for diffs.
*.wasm  binary
*.cwasm binary
*.wat   text eol=lf
*.wit   text eol=lf

# Vendored/lock data that must round-trip exactly.
*.lock  text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.toml  text eol=lf

# Scripts: LF, so they behave the same in CI containers as locally.
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.py    text eol=lf

# Windows-native files keep CRLF, because the tooling that reads them
# (PowerShell, cmd) is inconsistent about bare LF in some contexts.
*.ps1   text eol=crlf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf
*.bat   text eol=crlf

# Images and archives.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.zip   binary
*.gz    binary
